hi I'm Emily Ekins and I'm the polling  director at Reason Foundation we  recently conducted a poll of 696  California voters including 508 likely  voters we asked him about the  presidential election California ballot  initiatives and municipal pension and  higher education reform Californians are  concerned about the trajectory of the  state of California in fact fifty-eight  percent think it's off on the wrong  track between 2000 and 2010 the  California State Legislature increase  spending per capita by forty two percent  what surprised me was that a majority of  Californians actually thought this  increased spending decreased their  quality of life and a majority favored  rolling back spending to 2000 levels  proposition 30 is a temporary tax  increase on higher income families as  well as a sales tax increase with the  intent of raising about six billion  dollars in additional revenue for  education including higher education the  results are too close to call slightly  more in favor about fifty percent plan  to vote yes on proposition 30 and about  46 plan to vote no so we followed up  with respondents asking them if  proposition 30 were to pass but it did  not raise enough revenue to close the  deficit what would they like the state  legislature to do next seventy-four  percent want the legislature to cut  spending to balance the budget  Californians are not just concerned with  the level of spending but also about  regulations that the California  government is passing in fact two-thirds  of them think that most of the laws and  regulations passed drive jobs out of the  state of California so these are just  some of the results from our poll to  learn more about what Californians think  about the California state economy  government employee reform outsourcing  trash collection and the political bias  of higher education professors go to  reason calm / poll  you
 

